== Chapter III

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The seat or subject of the law of sin. The heart, and what is meant by it. Properties of the heart as possessed by sin. Unsearchable. Deceitful. Where that deceit comes from. Uses of these considerations.]
]

#smallcaps[Having] shown that #emph[indwelling sin], which we are treating in its remainders in believers, is a #emph[law], and having shown its power in general from this, we will now go on to give particular instances of its efficacy and advantages from some things that relate to it generally as such. And these are three: first, its #emph[seat] and subject; secondly, its #emph[natural] properties; and thirdly, its #emph[workings] and the manner of them, which is what we chiefly aim at and will attend to.

First, as for the #emph[seat] and subject of this law of sin, the Scripture everywhere declares it to be the #emph[heart]. There #emph[indwelling sin] keeps its special residence. It has invaded and possessed the throne of God himself (Eccl 9:3): “the hearts of men are full of … madness while they are alive.” This is their madness, or the root of all the madness that appears in their lives (Matt 15:19): “For out of the heart come evil thoughts, murder, adultery, sexual immorality, theft, false testimony, slander,” and so on. Many outward temptations and provocations befall men which excite and stir them up to these evils. But they only, as it were, open the vessel and let out what is laid up and stored in it. The root, rise, and spring of all these things is in the #emph[heart]. Temptations and occasions put nothing into a man; they only draw out what was in him before. From this comes that summary description of the whole work and effect of this law of sin in Gen 6:5: “Every inclination of the thoughts of man’s heart was altogether evil all the time”; so also in Gen 8:21. The whole work of the law of sin, from its first rise, from its first coining of actual sin, is described here, and its seat, its workhouse, is said to be the #emph[heart]. And so our Savior calls it “the evil treasure of the heart” (Luke 6:45): “the evil man brings evil things out of the evil treasure of his heart.” This #emph[treasure] is the prevailing principle of moral actions that is in men. So at the beginning of the verse our Savior calls #emph[grace] the #emph[good treasure] of the heart of a good man, from which what is good comes. It is a principle that constantly and #emph[abundantly] incites and stirs men up to actions agreeable to it and like it, of the same kind and nature as itself, and so brings them forth. And it is also called a #emph[treasure] because of its abundance. It will never be exhausted; it is not used up by men’s spending from it. Indeed, the more lavish men are with this stock, the more they draw out of this treasure, the more it grows and abounds. As men do not spend their grace but increase it by its exercise, no more do they spend their indwelling sin. The more men exercise their grace in duties of obedience, the more it is strengthened and increased. And the more men put out and bring forth the fruits of their lust, the more that lust is enraged and increased in them. It feeds on itself, swallows its own poison, and grows by it. The more men sin, the more they are inclined to sin. It is from the #emph[deceitfulness] of this law of sin, which we will speak of at length later, that men persuade themselves that by this or that particular sin they will so satisfy their lusts that they will not need to sin any more. Every sin increases the #emph[principle] and strengthens the habit of sinning. It is an #emph[evil treasure] that increases by doing evil. And where does this treasure lie? It is in the #emph[heart]\; there it is laid up, there it is kept safe. All the men in the world and all the angels in heaven cannot take this treasure out of a man’s possession, it is stored so safely in the heart.

The #emph[heart] is used in various ways in the Scripture: sometimes for the #emph[mind] and understanding, sometimes for the #emph[will], sometimes for the #emph[affections], sometimes for the #emph[conscience], sometimes for the whole soul. Generally it means the #emph[whole soul of man] and all its faculties, not absolutely, but as they are all one principle of moral working, as they all work together in our doing good or evil. The #emph[mind] as it inquires, discerns, and judges what is to be done and what refused; the #emph[will] as it #emph[chooses] or #emph[refuses] and avoids; the #emph[affections] as they #emph[like] or #emph[dislike], cling to or turn away in aversion from what is set before them; the #emph[conscience] as it warns and decides: all these together are called the #emph[heart]. And it is in this sense that we say the seat and subject of this law of sin is the #emph[heart] of man. Only we may add that when the Scripture speaks of the heart as the principle of men’s good or evil actions, it usually hints along with it at two things belonging to the manner in which they are performed.

First, a #emph[fitness] and pleasingness to the soul in the things that are done. When men take delight and are pleased in and with what they do, they are said to do it #emph[heartily], with their whole hearts. So when God himself blesses his people in love and delight, he says that he does it “with all My heart and with all My soul” (Jer 32:41).

Secondly, resolution and constancy in such actions. This also is meant by the #emph[metaphorical] expression used before, a #emph[treasure], from which men constantly take out the things they either need or intend to use.

This is the subject, the seat, the dwelling place of the law of sin: the #emph[heart], as it is the entire principle of moral workings, of doing good or evil, as good or evil come out of it. Here our enemy dwells. This is the fort, the #emph[citadel] of this tyrant, where it maintains a rebellion against God all our days. Sometimes it has more strength, and so more success; sometimes less of the one and of the other; but it is always in rebellion while we live.

So that we may take a brief look in passing at the strength and power of sin from this seat and subject of it, we may consider one or two properties of the heart that contribute greatly to it. It is like an enemy in war, whose strength and power lie not only in his #emph[numbers] and the force of his men or arms, but also in the unconquerable forts he possesses. And such is the #emph[heart] to this enemy of God and of our souls, as will appear from its properties, one or two of which will be mentioned.

First, it is #emph[unsearchable]. Jer 17:9, 10: “Who can know it? I, the #smallcaps[Lord], search the heart.” The heart of man is open to God alone. That is why he claims the honor of #emph[searching the heart] as peculiar to himself, and as fully declaring him to be God as any other glorious attribute of his nature. We do not know one another’s hearts; we do not know #emph[our own hearts] as we ought. There are many who do not know their hearts even as to their general bent and disposition, whether it is good or bad, sincere and sound, or corrupt and worthless. But no one knows all the secret #emph[intrigues], the windings and turnings, the acts and aversions of his own heart. Does anyone have the perfect measure of his own light and darkness? Can anyone know what acts of choice or aversion his will will bring forth when it is presented with the endless variety of objects it has to deal with? Can anyone trace all the changes of his affections? Do the secret springs of acting and refusing in the soul lie open before the eyes of any man? Does anyone know what the motions of his mind or will will be in this or that combination of things, with objects suited in such a way, such reasonings put forward, such an appearance of desirable things? All in heaven and earth except the #emph[infinite], all-seeing God are utterly ignorant of these things. In this #emph[unsearchable heart] the law of sin dwells. And much of its security, and so of its strength, lies in this: it is past our finding out. We fight with an enemy whose #emph[secret strength] we cannot discover, and whom we cannot follow into its hiding places. That is why we often find, when we are ready to think sin quite ruined, that after a while it was only out of sight. It has coverts and retreats in an #emph[unsearchable heart] where we cannot pursue it. The soul may persuade itself that all is well, when sin may be safe in the hidden darkness of the mind, which it is impossible for the man to look into, for whatever makes things manifest is #emph[light]. It may suppose that the #emph[will to sin] is utterly taken away, when there is still an unsearchable reserve kept for a more suitable object, a more vigorous temptation than the one it is tried with at present. Has a man had a contest with some lust, and a blessed victory over it by the Holy Spirit in that present trial? When he thinks it is utterly driven out, he soon finds that it had only withdrawn out of sight. It can lie so close in the darkness of the mind, in the unfitness of the will, in the disorder and carnality of the affections, that no eye can discover it. The best of our wisdom is only to watch its first appearances, to catch its first #emph[stirrings under the earth] and workings, and to set ourselves against them; for we cannot follow it into the secret corners of the #emph[heart]. It is true that there is still a relief in this case. He to whom the work of destroying the law of sin and the body of death in us is chiefly committed, namely the Holy Spirit, comes with his axe to the very root, and there is nothing in an unsearchable heart that is not open and naked to him (Heb 4:12). But from this we may see, by way of duty, what an enemy we have to deal with.

Secondly, as it is unsearchable, so it is #emph[deceitful], as in the place mentioned above: it is “deceitful above all things,” beyond all comparison. There is great #emph[deceit] in the dealings of men in the world: great deceit in their counsels and schemes concerning their affairs, private and public, and great deceit in their words and actions. The world is full of deceit and fraud. But all this is nothing to the #emph[deceit] that is in man’s heart #emph[toward himself]\; for that is the meaning of the expression in this place, and not deceit toward others. Now incomparable deceitfulness, added to #emph[unsearchableness], greatly adds to and increases the strength of the law of sin because of its seat and subject. I am not yet speaking of the deceitfulness of sin itself, but of the deceitfulness of the #emph[heart] where it is seated. Prov 26:25: “seven abominations fill his heart”; that is, not only many, but an absolute, complete number, as #emph[seven] signifies. And they are abominations that consist in deceitfulness, as the caution before it hints: “do not trust him.” For it is only #emph[deceit] that should keep us #emph[from trusting] a thing as far as its nature allows.

Now this #emph[deceitfulness] of the heart, by which it is greatly helped in harboring sin, lies chiefly in these two things.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, it abounds in #emph[contradictions], so that it cannot be found and dealt with by any constant rule and way of proceeding. There are some men who have much of this from their natural constitution, or from other causes in their way of life. They seem to be made up of #emph[contradictions]. At times they are very #emph[wise] in their affairs, at times very #emph[foolish]\; very open, and very reserved; very #emph[pliable], and very obstinate; very easy to be entreated, and very revengeful; all to a remarkable degree. This is generally thought a bad character, and it is seldom found except where it comes from some notable predominant lust. But in general, with respect to moral good or evil, duty or sin, it is so with the heart of every man: flaming hot and stone cold; weak, and yet stubborn; obstinate, and pliable. The frame of the heart is ready to #emph[contradict] itself every moment. Now you would think you had it fixed in such a frame, such a way; soon it is quite otherwise, so that no one knows what to expect from it. The rise of this is the disorder that sin has brought on all its faculties. God created them all in a perfect harmony and union. The mind and reason were in perfect subjection and subordination to God and his will. The will, in its choice of good, answered to what the mind discovered of it. The affections constantly and evenly followed the understanding and will. The mind’s subjection to God was the spring of the orderly and #emph[harmonious] motion of the soul and all the wheels in it. When that was disturbed by sin, the rest of the faculties moved across and against one another. The will does not choose the good that the mind discovers; the affections do not delight in what the will chooses; but all of them jar and #emph[clash], cross and rebel against each other. This is what we have gained by our falling from God. So at times the will leads and the judgment follows. Indeed, commonly the affections, which should wait on all the rest, get the sovereignty and draw the whole soul captive after them. And that is why, as I said, the heart is made up of so many #emph[contradictions] in its actions. At times the mind keeps its sovereignty, the affections are in subjection, and the will is ready for its duty. This puts a good face on things. Immediately the rebellion of the affections or the obstinacy of the will takes over and prevails, and the whole #emph[scene] is changed. This, I say, makes the heart #emph[deceitful] above all things. It does not agree with itself at all; it is not constant to itself; it has no order that it keeps to; it is under no certain and stable direction. Rather, if I may put it so, it turns round on itself, where often the #emph[feet] lead and guide the whole.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, its deceit lies in its #emph[large promises] at the first appearance of things. And this also comes from the same principle as the former. Sometimes the affections are #emph[touched] and worked on, the whole heart appears in a good frame, and everything promises to be well. In a little while the whole frame is changed. The mind was not affected or turned at all; the affections played their part a little and then left, and all the fair promises of the heart left with them. Now add this #emph[deceitfulness] to the #emph[unsearchableness] mentioned before, and we will find that the difficulty of dealing effectually with sin in its seat and throne, to say the least, is greatly increased. A deceiving and deceived heart: who can deal with it? Especially when we consider that the heart employs all its deceits in the service of sin and contributes them all to its furtherance. All the disorder in the heart, all its false promises and fair appearances, promote the interest and advantages of sin. That is why God warns the people to take care, lest “their own hearts should entice and deceive them.”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Who can name all the treacheries and deceits that lie in the heart of man? It is not for nothing that the Holy Spirit expresses it so: “It is deceitful above all things”: uncertain in what it does, and false in what it promises. And this, moreover, among other causes, is why in carrying on our war against sin we not only have the old work to go over and over, but always have new work while we live in this world: still new stratagems and tricks to deal with, as it will be wherever unsearchableness and deceitfulness have to be contended with.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[There are many other properties of this seat and subject of the law of sin which might be dwelt on to the same end and purpose, but that would turn us too far aside from our particular aim. So I will pass these over with a few considerations.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Let us never reckon that our work of contending against sin, of crucifying, mortifying, and subduing it, is at an end. The place where it lives is #emph[unsearchable]. When we may think we have thoroughly won the field, there is still some reserve remaining that we did not see, that we did not know of. Many conquerors have been ruined by their carelessness after a victory, and many have been spiritually wounded after great successes against this enemy. #emph[David] was. His great surprise into sin came after a long profession, many experiences of God, and a watchful keeping of himself from his iniquity. And this is partly why the profession of many has declined in their #emph[old age] or riper years, which must be spoken of more particularly later. They gave up the work of mortifying sin before their work was at an end. There is no way for us to pursue sin in its unsearchable dwelling place except by never ending our pursuit. And on this account the command of the apostle in Col 3:5 is as necessary for those who are near the end of their race to obey as for those who are only at the beginning of it: “Put to death, therefore, the components of your#super[+] earthly nature.” Be always doing it while you live in this world. It is true that great ground is gained when the work is carried on vigorously and constantly. Sin is much weakened, so that the soul presses on toward perfection. But still the work must be #emph[endless], I mean while we are in this world. If we give up, we will quickly see this enemy putting itself forth with new strength and vigor. Perhaps under some great affliction, perhaps in some eminent enjoyment of God, in the sense of the sweetness of blessed communion with Christ, we have been ready to say that there was an end of sin, that it was dead and gone forever. But have we not found the contrary by experience? Has it not shown that it had only withdrawn into some unsearchable recesses of the heart, as to its being in us and its nature, though it may be greatly weakened in its power? Let us reckon on it, then, that there is no way to have our work done except by always doing it. And he who dies #emph[fighting] in this warfare surely dies a #emph[conqueror].]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, since it has its residence in what is #emph[changeable], inconstant, #emph[deceitful] above all things, this calls for constant #emph[watchfulness] against it. An open enemy who deals by #emph[violence] only always gives some respite. You know where to find him and what he is doing, so that at times you may sleep quietly without fear. But against adversaries who deal by deceit and treachery (which are long swords, and reach at the greatest distance), nothing will give security except constant #emph[watchfulness]. In this case it is impossible for us to be too jealous, doubtful, suspicious, or watchful. The heart has a thousand tricks and deceits, and if we are off our watch in the least, we may be sure to be taken by surprise. That is why commands and warnings are given again and again for #emph[watching], for being circumspect, diligent, careful, and the like. There is no living for those who have to deal with an enemy “deceitful above all things” unless they persist in such a frame. All the cautions given in this case are necessary, especially this one: “remember not to believe.” Does the heart make fair promises? Do not rest on them, but say to the Lord Christ, “Lord, undertake for me.” Does the sun shine fair in the morning? Do not count on a fair day because of it; the clouds may rise and fall. Though the morning gives a fair appearance of calm and peace, turbulent affections may rise and cloud the soul with sin and darkness.]

Thirdly, then, commit the whole matter with all care and diligence to him who can #emph[search the heart] to the uttermost and knows how to forestall all its treacheries and deceits. In the things mentioned before lies our #emph[duty], but here lies our #emph[safety]. There is no treacherous corner in our hearts that he cannot search to the uttermost; there is no deceit in them that he cannot defeat. #emph[David] takes this course in Ps 139. After he has set out the #emph[omnipresence] of God and his #emph[omniscience] (verses 8, 9, 10), he makes use of it in #emph[verse] 23: “Search me, O God, … test me.” As if he had said, “I know only a little of my deceitful heart. Only I want to be #emph[sincere]\; I would not have any reserves for sin kept in it. So you who are present with my heart, who know my thoughts long before, undertake this work and perform it thoroughly, for you alone are able to do so.”

There are still other arguments that show the power and strength of #emph[indwelling sin], for which it is called a #emph[law], and we must go through them in the order in which we laid them down before.
